    "explanation": "The suprarenal (adrenal) glands are retroperitoneal organs\nlocated on the upper poles of the kidneys. The right gland is\npyramidal in shape, while the left is semilunar (crescentic).\n(Choice C) The left and right suprarenal glands\nrest posteriorly on the diaphragm\n(Choice D) The right (not the left) suprarenal gland extends\nmedially behind the inferior vena cava\nEach adrenal gland has two main regions: the cortex and the\nmedulla.\nThe medulla is derived from neural crest cells, the medulla\nsecretes epinephrine and norepinephrine directly into the\nbloodstream.\nThe adrenal cortex consists of three layers, each producing\nspecific steroid hormones:\nZona glomerulosa\n• Outermost layer\n• Produces mineralocorticoids, mainly aldosterone\n• Aldosterone regulates electrolyte and water balance\nZona fasciculata\n• Middle layer\n• Produces glucocorticoids, mainly cortisol and\ncorticosterone\n• Cortisol regulates glucose metabolism and\nsuppresses immune response\nZona reticularis\n• Innermost layer\n• Produces androgens (sex steroids)\n• Androgens contribute to the development of\nsecondary sex characteristics and libido in females\nRemember the hormones produced in each layer using\nthe mnemonic\nGo FoR MeGA!\n• Glomerulosa: Mineralocorticoids\n• Fasciculata: Glucocorticoids\n• Reticularis: Androgens",
