    "explanation": "If the membranous part of the urethra is ruptured, urine will\nescape into the deep perineal pouch (space) and can\nextravasate upward around the prostate and bladder or\ndownward into the superficial perineal pouch.\nIf the bulbous portion of the spongy urethra is ruptured,\nurine may pass into the superficial perineal space and\nspread to the following structures:\n• Inferiorly: scrotum\n• Anteriorly: around the penis\n• Superiorly: lower part of the abdominal wall\nUrine cannot spread posteriorly into the anal\nregion because the perineal membrane and Colles fascia are\ncontinuous with each other around the superfic",
