    "id": "medqbank-anatomy-0045",
    "subject": "anatomy",
    "originalNumber": 45,
    "stem": "The oculomotor nerve enters the orbit\nthrough which of the following routes?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Through the superior orbital\nfissure and the common\ntendinous ring"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Through the optic canal and the\ntendinous ring"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Through the orbital fissure but\noutside the tendinous ring"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "The oculomotor nerve does not\nenter the orbit"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "The following structures enter the orbit through the superior\norbital fissure and the common tendinous ring:\n• Oculomotor nerve\n• Nasociliary nerve\n• Abducens nerve\nThe common tendinous ring is a fibrous ring that surrounds\nthe optic canal and the medial part of the superior orbital\nfissure.\nIt is the site of origin of the four rectus muscles of the\neye and transmits the following structures:",
