    "subject": "anatomy",
    "originalNumber": 38,
    "stem": "A 39-year-old small business manager sustained a gunshot wound in the pelvic cavity, resulting in a lesion of the sacral splanchnic nerves. Which of the following nerve fibers would primarily be damaged?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Postganglionic sympathetic fibers"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Preganglionic sympathetic fibers"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Postganglionic parasympathetic fibers"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "The greater splanchnic nerves contain visceral afferent (VA) and preganglionic sympathetic visceral efferent (VE) fibers.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The sacral splanchnic nerves carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers that synapse in the inferior mesenteric ganglion. Postganglionic sympathetic fibers then continue to their target structures, including blood vessels, smooth muscle, and glands in the pelvic region.",
      "B": "The sacral splanchnic nerves are part of the sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system, and they carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers, not parasympathetic fibers. Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the pelvic viscera typically originate from the sacral segments of the spinal cord (pelvic splanchnic nerves).",
      "C": "The greater splanchnic nerves contain visceral afferent (VA) and preganglionic sympathetic visceral efferent (VE) fibers.",
      "D": "Postganglionic parasympathetic fibers are typically carried by the vagus nerve (cranial nerve X) in the pelvic region, not the sacral splanchnic nerves."
    },
