    "subject": "anatomy",
    "originalNumber": 65,
    "stem": "A 58-year-old man is admitted due to left flank pain. Blood exam indicates hematuria and anemia. MRI reveals that blood flow in the left renal vein is being occluded by an arterial aneurysm where the vein crosses the aorta. The aneurysm is MOST likely located in which artery?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Left colic"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Superior mesenteric"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Inferior mesenteric"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Celiac"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "The left renal vein courses between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery. Therefore, an aneurysm causing compression or occlusion of the left renal vein is MOST likely located in the superior mesenteric artery.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The left colic artery is not directly related to the left renal vein's course between the aorta and superior mesenteric artery.",
      "B": "The left renal vein courses between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery. Therefore, an aneurysm causing compression or occlusion of the left renal vein is MOST likely located in the superior mesenteric artery.",
      "C": "The inferior mesenteric artery is not typically associated with the course of the left renal vein.",
      "D": "The celiac artery, or celiac trunk, is not directly involved in the course of the left renal vein between the aorta and superior mesenteric artery."
    },
