    "id": "notion-anatomy-0081",
    "subject": "anatomy",
    "originalNumber": 81,
    "stem": "Coronary angiographs of a 43-year old male patient reveal an occlusion of the circumflex branch of the left coronary. This patient has been suffering from myocardial infarction in which of the following areas?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Right and Left Ventricles"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Right Atria and Left Atria"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Interventricular Septum"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Left Atrium and Ventricles"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "The left atrium and ventricles are supplied by the left circumflex artery, making them susceptible to infarction in case of occlusion in this vessel.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The left anterior descending artery (LAD) and the posterior interventricular artery primarily supply the right and left ventricles. The occlusion of the circumflex branch is less likely to directly impact the right ventricle.",
      "B": "The right atrium is mainly supplied by the right coronary artery (RCA), and the left atrium is supplied by the left circumflex artery. Occlusion of the circumflex branch may impact the blood supply to the left atrium but not directly to the right atrium.",
      "C": "The interventricular septum receives blood supply from both the LAD and the posterior interventricular artery. The occlusion of the circumflex branch may have some indirect effect on the interventricular septum.",
      "D": "The left atrium and ventricles are supplied by the left circumflex artery, making them susceptible to infarction in case of occlusion in this vessel."
    },
