    "id": "notion-anatomy-0084",
    "subject": "anatomy",
    "originalNumber": 84,
    "stem": "A 6-year old boy is admitted to the hospital with high fever and pain over the parotid gland. A diagnosis of parotitis (mumps) is established, and the boy is sent back home. Which of the following nerves is responsible for painful sensations from the region of the parotid gland?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Lingual"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Auricotemporal"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Chorda Tympani"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Lesser Petrosal"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "The auricotemporal nerve is a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). It closely relates to the parotid gland, supplying sensory fibers to the auricle and temporal region. Additionally, it sends articular fibers to the temporomandibular joint (TMJ).",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The lingual nerve is a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). While it does provide sensory fibers to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, it is not closely related to the parotid gland.",
      "B": "The auricotemporal nerve is a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). It closely relates to the parotid gland, supplying sensory fibers to the auricle and temporal region. Additionally, it sends articular fibers to the temporomandibular joint (TMJ).",
      "C": "The chorda tympani is a branch of the facial nerve (VII). It carries taste fibers from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and has a role in salivary gland innervation, specifically the submandibular and sublingual salivary glands.",
      "D": "The lesser petrosal nerve is a branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX). It is involved in the parasympathetic innervation of the parotid gland."
    },
