    "id": "notion-anatomy-0087",
    "subject": "anatomy",
    "originalNumber": 87,
    "stem": "A pediatric surgeon has resected a structure that is a fibrous remnant of an embryonic or fetal artery in a 5 year old child. Which of the following structures is MOST likely to be divided?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Ligamentum Teres Hepatis"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Lateral Umbilical fold"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Medial umbilical fold"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Ligamentum venosum"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "The ligamentum teres hepatis is the fibrous remnant of the fetal umbilical vein. It extends from the umbilicus to the liver and is a structure that typically closes after birth.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The ligamentum teres hepatis is the fibrous remnant of the fetal umbilical vein. It extends from the umbilicus to the liver and is a structure that typically closes after birth.",
      "B": "The lateral umbilical fold is formed by the inferior epigastric vessels and is not directly related to the fetal vasculature. It is a peritoneal fold that becomes more prominent in the adult.",
      "C": "The medial umbilical fold is formed by the obliterated umbilical artery. In the adult, it represents the fibrous remnant of the fetal umbilical artery. It runs from the bladder to the umbilicus.",
      "D": "The ligamentum venosum is the fibrous remnant of the fetal ductus venosus. It connects the left branch of the portal vein to the inferior vena cava. It is a structure that typically closes after birth."
    },
