    "id": "notion-anatomy-0088",
    "subject": "anatomy",
    "originalNumber": 88,
    "stem": "A 20 year old woman complains of numbness of the nasopharynx after surgical removal of the adenoid. A lesion of which of the following nerves would be expected?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Superior cervical ganglion"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "External laryngeal nerve"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Glossopharyngeal nerve"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Maxillary nerve"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "The glossopharyngeal nerve supplies sensory innervation to the mucosa of the upper pharynx, whereas the vagus nerve supplies sensory innervation to the lower pharynx and larynx.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The superior cervical ganglion is part of the sympathetic nervous system and is involved in the innervation of various structures, including blood vessels and some glands. It is not directly associated with the numbness of the nasopharynx.",
      "B": "The external laryngeal nerve is a branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, which itself is a branch of the vagus nerve (cranial nerve X). It innervates the cricothyroid muscle but is not directly related to the nasopharynx.",
      "C": "The glossopharyngeal nerve supplies sensory innervation to the mucosa of the upper pharynx, whereas the vagus nerve supplies sensory innervation to the lower pharynx and larynx.",
      "D": "The maxillary nerve (branch of the trigeminal nerve, cranial nerve V2) is primarily responsible for sensory innervation of the maxilla and part of the nasal cavity. It may be involved in nasal sensation, but it is not typically associated with the nasopharynx."
    },
