    "id": "superexam-anatomy-0315",
    "subject": "anatomy",
    "originalNumber": 315,
    "stem": "A 34 y/o female was brought to ER secondary to a diffuse\nabdominal pain. she was hypotensive, tachycardic at\npresentation. PE showed diffuse rigidity of the abdomen with\nboth rebound and direct tenderness prominent on the\nepigastric area. CT scan was done showing blood collection\nin the lesser sac. she was a smoker and alcoholic beverage\ndrinker. she always skip her meals because she want to loose\nweight and that she always take mefenamic acid almost\neveryday for her migraine.prior to onset, she claims to have\nrecurrent history of vague epigastric pain whenever she skip\nmeal and that there were occasions of nocturnal awakening\nsecondary to epigastric pain. what specific vascular structure\nwill most likely be affected in this setting?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "portal vein"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "gastroduodenal artery"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "(L) gastroepiploic artery"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "all of the above"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "based on the history, the patient is most likely to\nhave a perforated duodenal ulcer. The most\ncommon site for duodenal ulcer is its 1st part\nwhich is intraperitoneal structure. CT scan finding\nof blood in the lesser sac indicates that the\nperforation is most likely at the posterior wall.\ngastroduodenal artery is immediately behind the\n1st part of the duodenum. affectation of this\nstructure can lead to bleeding in the lesser sac,\nand sometimes pancreatitis. Snell 9th ed p 176",
